 Monday ✅.

 Today's newsletter is 763 words, a 3-minute read. 

 📉 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed down 0.5%.

 🔥 Today's stock spotlight: Intuitive Machines (+7.2%), the space company, said it received an "authorization to proceed" from an unnamed customer to start work on a $600 million, multisatellite communications infrastructure program.

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: OpenAI&#x27;s power play 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 OpenAI announced a lease for a sprawling data center in central Ohio with financing partly guaranteed by Nvidia, saying it will create 35,000 construction jobs and 2,500 permanent operating positions.

 Why it matters: The facility — which will be built and owned by SoftBank's SB Energy — illustrates the immensity of the computing and power needed to fuel the growth of the AI economy. Zoom in: The data center will have 8 IT-gigawatts of computing capacity, powered by 10 gigawatts of new energy generation, on private land and federal property formerly used for uranium enrichment.

 A massive 9.2 gigawatts of new gas-fired power is ultimately envisioned for the Ohio project — enough energy to power around 7 million homes, though estimates vary. Nvidia — which will be the exclusive provider of chips to the site — agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion in conditional lease and power payment obligations to SB Energy, according to an SEC filing . Nvidia also said today that it's investing $1.5 billion in SB Energy to back its "continued evolution into a leading AI infrastructure developer." What we're watching: The deal may reignite fears of a "circular" AI bubble that could create systemic financial risks.

 Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang took to X to try to head off the argument: "Is this circular financing? No. OpenAI will pay the lease," he said, adding that Nvidia is using "its scale and long-term visibility to" enable the site to happen. Go deeper 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 2. Anthropic surges 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Anthropic's second-quarter revenue exploded from a year ago, helping push the AI lab's annualized revenue run rate above $65 billion ahead of its expected IPO, according to figures reported by Bloomberg . 

 The metric — an estimate of future annual revenue based on a short period of data — was hit at the end of July, per Bloomberg , and implies a sevenfold increase from where it was at the end of last year, Axios' Madison Mills writes. What we're watching: Anthropic could go public before its rival, OpenAI.

 IPOs would help the companies tap additional pools of capital to secure more capacity for model usage — and going first could give the Claude maker a leg up. State of play: OpenAI's latest revenue run rate hit $40 billion, according to a message shared internally by co-founder Greg Brockman last week. 

 Caveat: The two companies may not measure revenue the same way. Reality check: The "winner" of any metric used to measure where each company is in the AI race changes constantly.

